---
Part XI, Chapter 13: The shell on paper
Package: packages/loom-design/src/
Language: CSS
---

# The shell on paper

The shell wears the site's own scheme, because Loom Design is part of Loom and a tool that looked like something else would say otherwise. Paper carries the chrome, the application sits on it as a white sheet, and the notes take the one dark ground this scheme allows.

Two rules from the site are load-bearing here and easy to lose. The base sets an open leading of two for running prose, so every piece of single-line chrome has to set its leading back to normal or it stands nearly twice as tall as its type. And nothing sets font smoothing, because switching a Mac from subpixel to grayscale thins every stroke on a page set in a monospace.

## The scheme, restated

The shell is a page of its own rather than a route of the site, so it declares the values it uses instead of reaching for a stylesheet it does not ship with. These are the site's, unchanged: three grounds, three steps of ink, two rules, the accent that marks a state, and the navy the one dark surface is built on.

Three colours name the kinds of note. They are the mark's own palette lifted for the dark ground they are printed on, so they run brighter than the same hues would on paper.

=>

:root {
  --paper:       #F7F4EC;
  --card:        #FCFAF4;
  --sheet:       #FFFFFF;

  --ink:         #14130F;
  --ink-2:       #4A453C;
  --ink-3:       #7C6F64;

  --rule:        #E4DCCB;
  --rule-faint:  #EFEAE0;

  --accent:      #E8501F;

  --navy:        #14204A;
  --navy-edge:   #2D3B6B;
  --navy-ink:    #F2EFE6;
  --navy-muted:  #8494C4;
  --navy-lift:   rgba(242, 239, 230, 0.06);

  --note-chat:   #6E9BFF;
  --note-dom:    #19D69C;
  --note-loom:   #A78BFA;

  --display: 'Space Grotesk', system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
  --mono:    'Lilex', 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace;

  --t-body:    15px;
  --t-code:    13px;
  --t-label:   12px;
  --t-eyebrow: 11px;

  --lead:      2;
  --radius:    4px;
  --radius-sm: 2px;
}

~

The page fills the window and never scrolls itself. Everything that scrolls does so inside the stage or inside the notes column, which is what keeps the bar in place and the frame's height honest.

=>

* { box-sizing: border-box; }

html, body {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  height: 100%;
  overflow: hidden;
}

body {
  background: var(--paper);
  color: var(--ink);
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-variant-ligatures: contextual common-ligatures;
  font-size: var(--t-body);
  line-height: var(--lead);
}

.shell {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  height: 100%;
}

## The bar

The bar is the site's nav doing a different job, so it keeps the type and loses the layout. Eleven pixels tracked wide in capitals and muted ink, fifty-six pixels tall, leading set back to normal — and then ranged left across the full width rather than centred on a measure, because a tool bar is a row of controls and not a masthead.

The wordmark is the one thing here in the display face, and the project name follows it in primary ink. The route reads as an address rather than a label: lower case, untracked, and free to be cut short when it runs long.

=>

.nav {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 14px;
  flex: none;
  height: 56px;
  padding: 0 16px;
  line-height: normal;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  background: var(--paper);
  font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
  letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ink-3);
}

.nav-word {
  flex: none;
  font-family: var(--display);
  font-weight: 500;
  font-size: 19px;
  letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  text-transform: none;
  color: var(--ink);
}

.shell-project {
  flex: none;
  padding: 3px 7px;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  background: var(--rule-faint);
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  text-transform: none;
  color: var(--ink-2);
}

.shell-route {
  min-width: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-size: var(--t-code);
  letter-spacing: 0;
  text-transform: none;
  color: var(--ink-3);
}

.shell-gap { flex: 1; }

~

The width pills and the two controls share the nav link's treatment, so the whole right end of the bar reads as one row. A chosen width and an open panel both take the faint rule behind them and full ink, which is exactly how the site marks the page a reader is already on.

The picker is the exception, and it takes the accent. The scheme names the Design picker among the things that accent is for — it marks a state the tool is in, not a place to go — so arming the picker is the one moment this bar changes colour.

=>

.shell-widths {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 2px;
  flex: none;
  margin-right: 6px;
}

.shell-width,
.shell-tool {
  appearance: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 6px;
  flex: none;
  padding: 5px 8px;
  border: none;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  background-color: transparent;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
  line-height: normal;
  letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ink-3);
  transition: background-color .14s ease, color .14s ease;
}

.shell-width { letter-spacing: 0.04em; }

.shell-width:hover,
.shell-tool:hover { background-color: var(--rule-faint); color: var(--ink); }

.shell-width.here,
.shell-tool.here { background-color: var(--rule-faint); color: var(--ink); }

.shell-tool.armed,
.shell-tool.armed:hover { background-color: rgba(232, 80, 31, 0.12); color: var(--accent); }

.shell-count {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
  padding: 0 4px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: var(--accent);
  font-size: 10px;
  letter-spacing: 0;
  color: var(--paper);
}

## The stage, and the sheet on it

The application is a white sheet on the paper, and nothing draws its edge. One soft shadow lifts it and the change of ground does the rest, which is how this site shows a document being written and the right way to show a page being worked on.

The stage centres the sheet with an automatic margin rather than by justifying its content. The difference only shows at a width wider than the stage: justified content is clipped on its left when it overflows and cannot be scrolled back to, while an automatic margin scrolls to both edges. A tool whose whole point is rendering at 1440 pixels in a smaller window has to get that right.

=>

.shell-body {
  display: flex;
  flex: 1;
  min-height: 0;
}

.shell-stage {
  display: flex;
  flex: 1;
  min-width: 0;
  padding: 20px;
  overflow: auto;
  background: var(--paper);
}

.shell-frame {
  display: block;
  flex: none;
  margin: auto;
  height: 100%;
  min-height: 320px;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: var(--sheet);
  box-shadow: 0 2px 26px rgba(20, 19, 15, 0.08);
}

.shell.picking .shell-frame { box-shadow: 0 2px 26px rgba(232, 80, 31, 0.22); }

~

The highlight is drawn over the whole page, above everything, because it belongs to the tool and not to the application. It takes the accent at two pixels with a wash inside it, and wears its name on a chip above its top-left corner. Nothing about it can be clipped or buried by the application, since the application is inside a frame and this is outside one.

The chip holds to forty characters and cuts from the left when it runs longer. A woven element names one section and always fits; an element with no anchor names a path down the tree, and the end of that path — the element itself, and the step above it — is the part worth reading. Cutting from the left is what keeps that end on screen.

=>

.shell-highlight {
  position: fixed;
  z-index: 40;
  pointer-events: none;
  border: 2px solid var(--accent);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  background: rgba(232, 80, 31, 0.08);
}

.shell-highlight-tag {
  position: absolute;
  left: -2px;
  top: -22px;
  max-width: 40ch;
  overflow: hidden;
  padding: 1px 6px;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  background: var(--accent);
  white-space: nowrap;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  direction: rtl;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
  line-height: normal;
  color: var(--paper);
}

## The notes, on the one dark ground

The panel is the search palette's navy card standing as a column. The site goes dark in exactly one place — where a reader has stopped reading the page and started working on it — and a column of notes about the page is that same moment, so it takes the same ground.

The head carries the two tabs, and a tab draws its rule only when chosen. Every tab reserves that rule in transparent, so the row's baseline never moves as the choice does.

=>

.shell-panel {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  flex: none;
  width: 360px;
  min-height: 0;
  background: var(--navy);
  border-left: 1px solid var(--navy-edge);
  color: var(--navy-ink);
}

.note-head {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 18px;
  flex: none;
  padding: 16px 18px 12px;
}

.note-tab {
  appearance: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 0 0 5px;
  border: 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
  background: transparent;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
  line-height: normal;
  letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--navy-muted);
  transition: color 120ms ease;
}

.note-tab.here { border-bottom-color: var(--navy-ink); color: var(--navy-ink); }

~

The list is the only band that scrolls, and it keeps the palette's thin scrollbar so a column of notes does not grow a heavy grey rail down its side.

A note gives its meta line first — the kind, what it points at, and the route it was left on — then the sentence. The three kinds carry their colours here, and the route sits at the right end where it stays out of the way of the words.

=>

.note-list {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow-y: auto;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
  scrollbar-width: thin;
  scrollbar-color: rgba(242, 239, 230, 0.2) transparent;
}

.note-list::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 6px; }
.note-list::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: transparent; }
.note-list::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background: rgba(242, 239, 230, 0.2);
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
}

.note-row {
  flex: none;
  padding: 12px 18px 14px;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(242, 239, 230, 0.07);
}

.note-row:first-child { border-top: none; }
.note-row:hover { background: var(--navy-lift); }

.note-meta {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
  margin-bottom: 6px;
  font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
  line-height: normal;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
}

.note-kind.chat { color: var(--note-chat); }
.note-kind.dom  { color: var(--note-dom); }
.note-kind.loom { color: var(--note-loom); }

.note-pointer {
  min-width: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--navy-ink);
}

.note-route {
  margin-left: auto;
  flex: none;
  color: var(--navy-muted);
}

.note-text {
  font-size: var(--t-code);
  line-height: 1.7;
  color: var(--navy-ink);
  text-wrap: pretty;
}

~

The three things a person does to a note stay invisible until the cursor reaches the note. A column of sentences should read as sentences, and a row of controls under every one of them would turn it into a table.

=>

.note-controls {
  display: flex;
  gap: 4px;
  margin-top: 8px;
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 140ms ease;
}

.note-row:hover .note-controls { opacity: 1; }

.note-do {
  appearance: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 2px 6px;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  background: transparent;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
  line-height: normal;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  transition: background-color 120ms ease, color 120ms ease;
}

.note-do.quiet { color: var(--navy-muted); }
.note-do.quiet:hover { background: var(--navy-lift); color: var(--navy-ink); }
.note-do.done { color: var(--note-dom); }
.note-do.done:hover { background: rgba(25, 214, 156, 0.12); }

.note-empty {
  padding: 40px 18px;
  text-align: center;
  font-size: var(--t-code);
  line-height: 1.7;
  color: var(--navy-muted);
}

.note-banner {
  flex: none;
  padding: 12px 18px;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 197, 61, 0.24);
  background: rgba(255, 197, 61, 0.08);
  font-size: var(--t-code);
  line-height: 1.7;
}

.note-banner-head { color: #FFC53D; }
.note-banner-say { color: var(--navy-muted); }

## Writing a note

The composer holds the bottom of the column and never scrolls with the list above it. Its field takes a ground barely lifted off the navy, and the accent takes the focus ring, since being focused is a state and the accent is what marks one.

The send button is the mark's green, which on this ground is the one colour that reads as a thing the reader does rather than a thing the panel is saying.

=>

.note-composer {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 8px;
  flex: none;
  padding: 12px 18px 16px;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--navy-edge);
}

.note-aimed {
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
  line-height: normal;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  color: var(--accent);
}

.note-field {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 68px;
  padding: 9px 10px;
  border: 1px solid var(--navy-edge);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  outline: none;
  resize: none;
  background: var(--navy-lift);
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-code);
  line-height: 1.7;
  color: var(--navy-ink);
  transition: border-color 120ms ease;
}

.note-field::placeholder { color: #6C7CAE; }
.note-field:focus { border-color: var(--accent); }

.note-send-row,
.note-edit-actions { display: flex; gap: 8px; }

.note-send,
.note-save {
  appearance: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  flex: 1;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  background: var(--note-dom);
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-label);
  line-height: normal;
  color: var(--navy);
  transition: filter 120ms ease;
}

.note-send:hover,
.note-save:hover { filter: brightness(1.06); }

.note-cancel {
  appearance: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  flex: 1;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  border: 1px solid var(--navy-edge);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  background: transparent;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-label);
  line-height: normal;
  color: var(--navy-muted);
  transition: border-color 120ms ease, color 120ms ease;
}

.note-cancel:hover { border-color: #8FA0D6; color: var(--navy-ink); }

.note-edit {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 8px;
  flex: none;
  padding: 12px 18px 14px;
}

.note-jump {
  appearance: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  position: absolute;
  right: 18px;
  top: -44px;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 32px;
  height: 32px;
  border: 1px solid var(--navy-edge);
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: var(--navy);
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: var(--t-code);
  line-height: normal;
  color: var(--navy-muted);
  box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(11, 18, 48, 0.4);
  transition: color 120ms ease;
}

.note-jump:hover { color: var(--navy-ink); }

## When the window is narrow

A phone gets the frame and nothing else standing beside it. The panel stops being a column and becomes a sheet across the bottom half, the width pills go — a window this size is already the width being tested — and the bar keeps only what it needs to.

This is the shape that makes the shell usable on a tablet propped beside a laptop. It is not the shape that replaces the unframed path on a phone, which exists for what a frame cannot show at all: the safe area, and the print.

=>

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .shell-widths { display: none; }
  .shell-body { flex-direction: column; }
  .shell-stage { padding: 12px; }
  .shell-panel {
    width: 100%;
    max-height: 52%;
    border-left: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--navy-edge);
  }
  .shell-frame { width: 100% !important; }
}

