## The page {Tangle}[shell.html] {html}

The page is nearly empty, because the shell draws itself. It carries three things the shell reads or needs: the project the proxy resolved, written into an attribute since only the server knows which application it was pointed at; the stylesheet; and the script.

The two faces come from the same place the site's do. Neither is required — both stacks end in a face every system already has — so a shell opened without a network reads in the fallback rather than not at all.

=>

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" />
<title>Loom Design</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@500&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500;600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/__loom/shell.css" />
</head>
<body data-loom-project="__LOOM_PROJECT__">
  <div id="shell"></div>
  <script type="module" src="/__loom/shell.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
